import json, time
T = 1762560000000  # fixed so re-adding the pack never duplicates or reorders
topics = [
 dict(id="reservation", label="Reservation", color="#7a3fb0", desc="Reservation, caste and discrimination in India: three conversations with Vikas Divyakirti", parent=""),
 dict(id="reservation-what-why", label="What and why", color="#0e8fa3", desc="Lecture: reservation explained from first principles (equality, equity, merit)", parent="reservation"),
 dict(id="reservation-nehru-ambedkar", label="Nehru, Ambedkar & UGC rules", color="#c2417a", desc="ANI podcast with Smita Prakash: UGC equity rules, Ambedkar, Nehru and caste discrimination", parent="reservation"),
 dict(id="reservation-generations", label="Generations & casteism", color="#6250d6", desc="Clip: should benefits stop after a generation, creamy layer, caste vs casteism", parent="reservation"),
]
A = "reservation-what-why"; B = "reservation-nehru-ambedkar"; C = "reservation-generations"
N = []
def n(topic, key, title, text): N.append(dict(id="res-"+key, topic=topic, title=title, text=text))

# ---- A: lecture "Reservation: what and why" ----
n(A,"a01","Talking about reservation calmly","The speaker calls reservation the most controversial topic in India and asks listeners to hold two things: he does not personally receive reservation, and he supports the *theory* of reservation while thinking today's practice has many flaws. He wants people on both sides to argue from facts. (≈2:37)\n\nSee [[Confirmation bias]] and [[Veil of ignorance]]. #method")
n(A,"a02","What reservation covers","Reservation means a claim on jobs and seats: government jobs, admission to colleges, and political seats in Lok Sabha, assemblies and Panchayati Raj. The real questions are who should be covered, on what facts, how much, and for how long. (≈5:11, 17:00)\n\nLinks: [[Resources and why people get angry]], [[Vertical and horizontal reservation]]. #basics")
n(A,"a03","Resources and why people get angry","Reservation is about distributing resources, and anger comes when people feel something is being taken from them. The farm-bill protests are used as a parallel: when a policy seems to take away resources, a strong reaction is natural. Both sides of the reservation debate are defending what they feel is theirs. (≈6:33)\n\n#conflict")
n(A,"a04","Confirmation bias","We collect evidence for what we already believe and stop listening to opposing ideas. A person can be so sure that nothing will change their mind. The speaker asks listeners, supporters and opponents alike, to notice this before debating. (≈9:13)\n\nCounter-tools: [[Veil of ignorance]], [[Neutrality and scientific reflection]]. #bias")
n(A,"a05","Veil of ignorance","Idea of John Rawls: design the rules as if you did not know your own place in society. Then you would not defend a system just because it favours you, and you would see where you are quietly benefiting or being harmed. (≈13:07)\n\nUsed to frame [[Equality, equity and distribution]]. #ethics")
n(A,"a06","Neutrality and scientific reflection","Look at a topic without bringing in what your religion, country or family says. Be fair and objective, weigh advantages and disadvantages, and ask what is right and what is wrong, not who is saying it. (≈14:23)\n\n#method")
n(A,"a07","Equality, equity and distribution","A family with two children, one weak: giving both a glass of milk is equality, but the weak child needs more for a while to recover. Once recovered, the extra stops. The speaker uses this to show that equal treatment can be unjust when starting points differ, and that special help can be temporary. (≈23:31)\n\nFollow-ups: [[Level playing field: basketball and wrestling]], [[Negative and positive equality]]. #core")
n(A,"a08","Level playing field: basketball and wrestling","If two players have very different heights, the same hoop is not a fair test, and wrestling uses weight classes so that fights are fair. Fair competition sometimes means adjusting conditions, not ignoring differences. (≈28:50, 31:21)\n\n#analogy")
n(A,"a09","Two children and the IAS race","A child from a metro city with good schools, guidance and money versus a child from a village with a weak school, no coaching and early work. Putting both in the same exam and calling it equal ignores the 20 years before the exam. Equal rules over unequal starts give unequal results. (≈32:39–39:10)\n\nSee [[Negative and positive equality]] and [[Meritocracy and environment]]. #core")
n(A,"a10","Negative and positive equality","Negative equality means no one has special privileges any more (no discrimination by caste, religion, gender). Positive equality goes further: some protective discrimination to bring the disadvantaged into the mainstream. The speaker says that when someone says reservation is against equality, this second stage is what they miss. (≈41:47–52:09)\n\n#core")
n(A,"a11","Exploitation versus protective discrimination","Discrimination that exploits a group (slavery, caste rules, barring women) is different from discrimination that protects: seats for the elderly or women on a bus, or reservation. The test is who is being helped and whether the aim is to end the disadvantage. (≈46:58–48:13)\n\nCompare [[Social discrimination and Ambedkar]]. #principle")
n(A,"a12","Women's participation","Women are under 5 percent in some of India's institutions of power and a small share in business and civil services. The speaker says this does not mean women are less capable; it reflects long denied opportunity and rights. (≈57:25–58:43)\n\n#example")
n(A,"a13","Social discrimination and Ambedkar","Caste and religion produce discrimination at a social level, not only between individuals. Dr Ambedkar's account of being refused water at school and the Channar revolt in Travancore (women of lower castes barred from covering the upper body, taxed if they did) are told as examples of structural exploitation. (≈1:02:41–1:14:20)\n\nContinues in [[Documented discrimination]] in the podcast subtopic. #history")
n(A,"a14","Welfare state and affirmative action","A welfare state must bring weaker groups into the mainstream. The US started affirmative action in the 1960s, courts later allowed race to count as a factor but struck down hard quotas, and many developed countries have similar arrangements. (≈1:16:56, 1:43:09–1:49:35)\n\n#comparison")
n(A,"a15","Bonus points versus quotas","Two ways to help: add a small number of marks for disadvantaged categories (a soft preference, like the US approach), or reserve seats (a quota, as in India). The speaker explains both with a numerical selection example. (≈1:19:36–1:24:48)\n\n#mechanism")
n(A,"a16","Vertical and horizontal reservation","Vertical reservation is for SC, ST and OBC; horizontal reservation cuts across them for women and persons with disabilities. Candidates who qualify on merit go to the open list first, and the reserved seats are filled after that. (≈1:32:30–1:41:51)\n\nSee [[What reservation covers]]. #mechanism")
n(A,"a17","Caste census and missing data","India's last full caste count was 1931. The Mandal Commission worked from that, and the Supreme Court has asked how backwardness can be decided without data. The speaker argues for data so that policy can be targeted. (≈1:26:06–1:29:56)\n\nCompare [[Data-driven proactive measures]]. #data")
n(A,"a18","Arguments for reservation","1) Bring disadvantaged groups into the mainstream. 2) In a democracy every community should take part roughly in proportion to its size. 3) When all groups share in the state, they trust nation-building more. (≈1:54:39–1:55:54)\n\nCounter-side: [[Arguments against reservation]]. #arguments")
n(A,"a19","Arguments against reservation","Common objections: rich people within reserved groups take most benefits ([[Creamy layer]]); people today are not responsible for their ancestors' acts; and merit and national competitiveness may suffer (a biotechnology race example). The speaker answers each in turn. (≈1:57:09–2:05:00)\n\n#arguments")
n(A,"a20","Meritocracy and environment","Merit is not purely inborn. It comes from genes interacting with family, school, community and the facilities a child gets, and a rich family keeps producing merit generation after generation. Tests such as IQ tests also reflect the test-makers' environment. So 'merit' alone cannot be the only fair basis. (≈2:02:25–2:15:24)\n\nLinks to [[Two children and the IAS race]]. #merit")
n(A,"a21","Creamy layer","The speaker agrees that some who get reservation are well off and that benefit can be taken by the better-off inside a reserved group, which breeds resentment. His answer is a policy with limits: benefit for those who are really deprived and not for generation after generation. (≈1:57:09, 2:20:32)\n\nSee the debate in [[Should benefits stop after one generation?]] and [[SC/ST sub-classification]]. #creamy-layer")
n(A,"a22","A balanced policy","The speaker says he is a middle-path person: do not go so far that society stays divided, and do not drop merit either. Reform the system (schools, early support), manage misuse, set time limits and review. Resentment is the state's job to manage. (≈2:16:41–2:24:26)\n\n#conclusion")

# ---- B: podcast "Nehru, Ambedkar & caste discrimination" (UGC rules) ----
n(B,"b01","UGC equity rules 2026","New University Grants Commission equity regulations on discrimination in campuses led to protests and a Supreme Court hearing. The speaker walks through the text, says some of the fear is understandable, and that a line or two of change could remove much of the opposition. (≈0:00–10:28)\n\n#policy")
n(B,"b02","Rohith Vemula and Payal Tadvi case","A 2019 petition by the mothers of Rohith Vemula and Payal Tadvi, both students who died by suicide, is the Supreme Court case behind the push for stronger rules on campus discrimination. (≈7:50)\n\nBackground to [[UGC equity rules 2026]]. #case")
n(B,"b03","Drafting gaps in the regulations","The speaker points out drafting problems: how OBC is placed in the definition, the general category not being named in some places, and careless proofreading for such an emotional issue. Clear text matters because people will read every word. (≈9:11–13:05)\n\n#drafting")
n(B,"b04","Equity committee and ombudsman","Each institution forms an equity committee led by senior faculty with representation of SC, ST, OBC, women, disabled and third gender members, and a complainant can appeal to an ombudsman. The speaker suggests adding a line so that representation works in states like Mizoram where the local majority differs. (≈14:20–22:08)\n\nTimeline: [[Fast timelines and fake complaints]]. #structure")
n(B,"b05","Fast timelines and fake complaints","The committee must act within 24 hours and report in days, with appeals within 30 days. Critics fear the accused is treated as guilty before proof, as with earlier special laws. The speaker compares past court balancing (SC/ST Act, 498A) and says a clear matrix and safeguards are needed. (≈14:20, 1:09:38–1:16:08)\n\nSee [[Process as punishment]]. #concern")
n(B,"b06","Process as punishment","If a student is charged and must prove innocence, months pass, so the process itself becomes the punishment. The speaker raises this about students aged 18 and above and asks for care so that real discrimination is dealt with and false cases do not ruin people. (≈1:10:58–1:13:32)\n\n#concern")
n(B,"b07","Vacant reserved seats","Many reserved faculty posts stay vacant. Dropout of SC/ST candidates is often due to discrimination and finances, and the older 'not found suitable' rule converted vacancies to general; carry-forward rules changed this. (≈24:39–25:55)\n\n#implementation")
n(B,"b08","Interview bias and machines","University lecturer selection is mostly an interview with a few people, which leaves room for bias. The speaker suggests recorded interviews and, in a few years, machine scoring of answer sheets as a fairer check than humans. (≈27:10–31:03)\n\n#selection")
n(B,"b09","Documented discrimination","Ambedkar writes that as a senior official in Baroda he could not find a house and was thrown out of a Parsi dharamshala when his caste was known. A report on AIIMS found a large share of SC/ST students faced differential treatment, such as hostel floors and exam copies. (≈33:39–38:43)\n\nSee [[Social discrimination and Ambedkar]] in the lecture subtopic. #evidence")
n(B,"b10","Beneficiary without guilt","I am not responsible for what my ancestors did, but I did benefit from earlier advantages and should be socially sensitive. The speaker says this applies to anyone, and that acknowledging privilege is not an accusation. (≈39:59–43:52, 50:14)\n\nAnswers [[Arguments against reservation]]. #ethics")
n(B,"b11","Systemic exploitation made invisible","Bonded labour that existed within living memory and the Minimum Wages Act (pushed in the Viceroy's council by Ambedkar) show how exploitation becomes normal and invisible to those who benefit. Deprived groups are often betrayed by the system, not only by individuals. (≈45:08–47:41)\n\n#history")
n(B,"b12","Invisible privilege of caste","Upper-caste people in cities often say they do not see caste. But surveys of hiring, top positions and matrimonial ads show it persists, with marriage within caste as the clearest sign. Not noticing caste is itself a privilege. (≈51:32–56:39)\n\n#privilege")
n(B,"b13","Thought experiment: the Dalit girl","Imagine being born as a Dalit girl in a small village of Uttar Pradesh, or a child of a labourer who works from age 12. How would you pass the exam? The speaker asks listeners to imagine this before judging. (≈57:55–59:12)\n\nSame spirit as [[Veil of ignorance]]. #empathy")
n(B,"b14","Implicit discrimination and legal language","Law must name unfair differential treatment, including implicit bias: for instance not shaking someone's hand because of caste and calling it 'personal choice'. The speaker also notes the regulations list grounds of discrimination in one paragraph and caste-based discrimination in another. (≈1:01:47–1:08:21)\n\n#law")
n(B,"b15","Models from other countries","Norway invests in training teachers and does not depend on regulations. US campuses have started adding caste to their policies after complaints, including at California State University. (≈1:19:58–1:23:46)\n\nCompare [[Welfare state and affirmative action]]. #comparison")
n(B,"b16","Dominant OBC castes and the buffer zone","OBC includes powerful land-owning castes (Yadavs, Jats, Marathas) and many small artisan castes. Following M. N. Srinivas, dominant castes hold land, numbers and political power. In north India they act as a buffer between upper castes and Dalits, so clashes were fewer than in the south; Mandal shaped OBC-centred politics. (≈1:34:19–1:42:03)\n\n#sociology")
n(B,"b17","Data-driven proactive measures","The rules require institutions to publish reports twice a year on student and staff composition and dropouts, and to act on them; non-compliance can cost grants and degree-granting powers. A jump in dropout of SC/ST students in one college is a signal to look for discrimination. (≈1:48:36–1:55:07)\n\nSee [[Caste census and missing data]]. #data")
n(B,"b18","Vote bank","The speaker says parties think in vote banks: the upper-caste voter base in one party, OBC centred politics in others, and fear is used to hold groups together. (≈1:56:25–2:00:18)\n\nSee [[Fear as persuasion]] and [[Batenge to katenge: two readings]]. #politics")
n(B,"b19","SC/ST sub-classification","Whether to divide SC/ST into sub-groups and give less to the better-off has been debated since the Constitution. The 2004 EV Chinnaiah ruling said no; the 2024 seven-judge bench (6:1) in Davinder Singh v. State of Punjab said states can. (≈2:02:52–2:04:07)\n\nRelated: [[Creamy layer]] and [[Should benefits stop after one generation?]]. #court")
n(B,"b20","Caste vs casteism","Caste is a social structure; casteism is the belief or politics built on it. Caste is gradually weakening in cities as occupation and caste separate, but casteism may continue as long as it is useful for votes. (≈2:05:27–2:09:23)\n\nSee also [[How long will caste last?]]. #sociology")
n(B,"b21","Nehru, caste and the First Amendment","The speaker discusses how Nehru and other leaders saw caste, mentioning the 1951 First Amendment after the Champakam Dorairajan case, and says casteism can also sit at the subconscious level: the way caste names are used in public discourse and in how leaders are described. (≈2:13:18–2:17:15)\n\n#history")

# ---- C: clip "Generations & casteism" ----
n(C,"c01","Should benefits stop after one generation?","If someone got into the IAS with reservation, should their children also get the benefit? In EWS and OBC, benefits usually stop after a generation through the creamy layer. SC/ST has no such limit. The speaker says that if asked, he would limit it to one or two generations, with some limits for the same family. (≈0:00–0:36, 3:27–3:45)\n\nSee [[Creamy layer]]. #generations")
n(C,"c02","Creamy layer","In OBC and EWS an officer's children fall into the creamy layer, and people sometimes find ways round it, such as resigning or showing only agricultural income. Genuine cases are rare because becoming that rich is hard. The speaker says the government should regulate these loopholes. (≈0:44–1:28)\n\nLinks: [[SC/ST sub-classification]]. #creamy-layer")
n(C,"c03","SC/ST sub-classification","The Indira Sawhney (Mandal) case of 1992 made removing the creamy layer compulsory for OBC. The 2004 EV Chinnaiah case said no sub-classification in SC/ST, but the 2024 judgment (Davinder Singh v. State of Punjab, seven judges, 6:1) allows states to do so. SC/ST lists are made by the Centre, OBC lists by states too. (≈1:31–3:12)\n\nCounter-view: [[Arguments against reservation]]. #court")
n(C,"c04","Will any government decide this?","The government could now limit generations in SC/ST, but all democratic governments act with political benefit in mind. The speaker says there must be some limit, so the same family does not keep getting the benefit while others from the same community get none. (≈3:07–3:45)\n\n#politics")
n(C,"c05","Batenge to katenge: two readings","The slogan 'if you divide, you will be cut' is read in two ways: as anti-Muslim commentary, or as a call for Hindus to unite beyond caste. The speaker asks whether Hindu unity could mean the end of caste discrimination, and notes it is not an annulment of caste. (≈3:45–4:30, 9:31–9:42)\n\nSee [[Vote bank]] and [[Caste vs casteism]]. #politics")
n(C,"c06","How long will caste last?","Citing M. N. Srinivas, the speaker thinks caste will not go quickly; a conservative estimate is at least a thousand years, though it fades gradually, first in cities where you may not care about a colleague's caste. In marriage it persists. (≈4:34–5:21)\n\n#sociology")
n(C,"c07","Caste and occupation have separated","In the last 100 years the link between caste and occupation has weakened. Brahmins now work in every field, leather work has become a shoe business, and a barber who opens a salon becomes a hairstylist with a new status. Acting and classical dance, once low, are now honoured. (≈5:24–7:26)\n\n#change")
n(C,"c08","Intercaste marriage trend","The speaker estimates around 10 percent of marriages are intercaste now and says it might reach 25 percent in a hundred years and more after that. In large cities it is no longer rare. (≈7:26–7:49)\n\nRough personal estimate, not a measured figure. #marriage")
n(C,"c09","Caste vs casteism","Caste is one thing; casteism is another. Caste-based politics can be run by people who do not believe in caste themselves, because they have a vote bank. Political casteism is not the same as social casteism, and casteism continues until others see it. (≈7:49–9:01)\n\nSee [[Batenge to katenge: two readings]]. #sociology")
n(C,"c10","Vote bank","A vote bank is an interest or pressure group built on ethnicity, caste, religion or economics. Rajput leaders may unite Rajputs for votes and later bring Dalits in when needed. Religion is also a vote bank, so cutting across caste does not annul caste. (≈8:07–9:41)\n\nCompare [[Dominant OBC castes and the buffer zone]] and [[Fear as persuasion]]. #politics")
n(C,"c11","Voting on issues versus identity","In democracy each individual should vote by conscience, but in practice people form groups. In developed countries voting blocs form around issues such as poverty and health care; here they form around caste and religion. Norway is mentioned as a model. (≈9:02–10:22)\n\n#democracy")
n(C,"c12","Fear as persuasion","To persuade someone, go through emotion first, and fear is the strongest. The speaker gives US examples (parties reminding voters of threats) and says marketing psychology works the same way, so fear is used among general-category voters. (≈10:22–11:06)\n\n#psychology")

